# Lead corrective design: selected JUnit4 metadata and output bounds

Task: `android-studio-task/1-junit4-declarations`, own worktree only. Current
module SHA-256: `739d29d71996845ac671ea870d266383cf3cba73b4c9b910537d515267ac3aec`.
No production edits implement this proposal yet. The history remains three
owner static fix rounds plus two authorized lead test/helper corrections. The
current 108 tests and strict Clippy pass; performance is FAIL and task incomplete.
Canonical 92 rows, pinned manifest, prior discovery, all 16 original fixtures
(100,711 bytes) and all existing 34 new test assertions remain unchanged.

## Findings and acceptance

1. A 1 MiB package, one class and 512 annotated methods can retain about
   513 MiB of owners before serialization/output-limit rejection.
2. A 1 MiB annotation literal before a class bypasses the current 16 KiB
   class-to-brace bound when the expanded header is copied.
3. Current `to_vec_pretty` allocates all output before checking its size.

The proposed correction must charge owned evidence strings before allocation,
share that charge across the complete selection, reject JSON writes before
extending a buffer beyond the existing 16 MiB output cap, and enforce the
existing 16 KiB class-header cap on the expanded annotation/modifier prefix.
No limit increase, framework-binding expansion, automatic N/A or parity credit.
Source budget failure rejects generation with an explicit unresolved-evidence
error; it must never produce an apparently complete partial output. A header
cap failure retains original file provenance/global annotation facts and the
existing unresolved class/header reason; affected methods cannot gain direct
ownership/confirmation. Runner counts remain null and applicability unreviewed.

## Exact implementation scope

Only the new module, its NEW stress/boundary tests and its task documentation
change. No dependency/workspace manifests, existing assertions, original source
fixtures, selection identities, publication code or canonical ledger change.

### One aggregate string budget

Add `MetadataBudget { used: usize, limit: usize }`, production limit equal to
`MAX_TOTAL_BYTES` (16 MiB). `charge(additional)` uses checked arithmetic and
rejects before increasing used beyond limit. No refund makes accounting
conservative, including dropped temporaries/duplicate set insertions.

Create the budget once in `generate`, before parsing bounded manifest/selection.
Charge each bounded JSON input's byte length before deserialization, conservatively
covering its decoded owned strings. Those reads themselves keep their existing
256 KiB input caps. Charge additional identity-key clones, reference field
clones and generated evidence hashes before allocation. Move existing selected
fields/baseline/scope without recopying them. Files share the SAME budget; do not
reset it in the loop. Existing raw source reads remain capped independently.

Pass `&mut MetadataBudget` into a fallible scanner and its owned-string helpers.
Keep the existing `source_facts(text, kotlin)` test convenience wrapper, with a
normal budget and an explicit expectation that existing small inputs succeed;
production uses the fallible budget-sharing scanner. Budget exhaustion returns
an error such as `selected declaration metadata budget exhausted; declaration
evidence unresolved and not published`, propagated by `generate` before staging.
The scanner does not publish partial facts on budget exhaustion.

Audit every scanner allocation site (`to_owned`, `format!`, `clone`) involving
owned evidence strings, including package/import/alias names, annotation
spelling/use-site targets, copied annotation facts, class names/kinds/owners/
headers, method names/owners/signature hashes and unresolved reasons. Central
helpers must reserve string lengths before construction:

- `owned(&str)` charges length then copies; `clone_annotation` charges all its
  owned strings before copying. Fixed reason strings are charged too, including
  those currently created by tokenization (make tokenization fallible with the
  same budget, or return borrowed static reasons and charge before ownership).
- `qualified_name` first walks borrowed tokens to determine end/checked output
  length, then charges and builds once. Do not allocate its whole String and
  charge afterward. No unbounded formatter temporary.
- Package-qualified owners compute checked `package.len() + 1 + name.len()`
  and charge before `format!`/String construction. Method-owner clones charge
  source owner length before cloning. Signature hash text reserves its fixed
  64 bytes before hex formatting.
- Change declaration-prefix collection to borrowed annotation facts or indices.
  Compute its full span before copying any header; budgeted annotation clones
  occur only after the appropriate declaration/header guards.
- Keep bounded structural token/delimiter/class/method collections and existing
  semantic checks. These structures are not claimed to be covered by the
  string-byte cap; their existing independent count limits still apply.

### Full class-header guard

After identifying the expanded prefix start, check the full borrowed slice from
that start up to (excluding) the class opening brace before any header `to_owned` or
annotation clones. Use the existing `MAX_HEADER_BYTES` value and existing
inclusive boundary convention. Class-to-brace and class-count guards remain.
On overflow continue with the existing explicit class/header/ownership unresolved
reason, preserving the original global annotation/source evidence. No owner is
created for an unsupported header and no method in it is confirmed by an
accidental neighboring owner.

### Bounded pretty JSON writer

Remove intermediate per-FileEvidence `to_vec_pretty` and its post-allocation
check. Aggregate string accounting already bounds retained evidence; final output
has its own strict cap. Serialize once with `serde_json::to_writer_pretty` into
`BoundedJsonWriter`, an `io::Write` implementation owning its Vec.

Before any write, calculate `len.checked_add(buf.len())`; reject if it exceeds
the existing 16 MiB cap. Only then reserve/grow and extend. Growth is geometric
but capped at the limit, with checked arithmetic and `try_reserve_exact`, so
small outputs do not repeatedly reallocate once per serializer fragment and no
requested Vec capacity exceeds the output cap. Allocation errors propagate.
Include the trailing newline through the same bounded writer, not a later
unchecked `push`. The completed Vec is returned only after serialization and
newline succeed. There is no full oversized intermediate JSON allocation.

Generation remains ahead of all publication staging; existing output and
`--check` behavior remain transactional. A serializer error causes no output
creation/replacement or staging leak. Successful JSON schema/order/pretty style
and newline stay byte-identical for the existing original selection; verify
against a preserved pre-correction selected output once a matching CLI can be
built, or compare bounded serialization with the original serializer for a small
unit value. No false archive authentication flag or capability claims change.

## New meaningful Rust tests (preserve every existing test)

1. **Aggregate budget arithmetic/allocation boundary:** an exact-length owned
   copy succeeds; one additional byte fails without changing usage. Checked
   arithmetic overflow fails. Charging a failed copy does not allocate it.
2. **Actual owner-amplification shape:** the review's 1 MiB Java package plus
   512 distinct `@Test void testN()` methods fits source/token limits. The
   fallible scanner with the normal 16 MiB shared budget returns a metadata-
   budget error before retaining all repeated owners; used never exceeds cap.
   A small corresponding source still retains declared evidence/runtime nulls.
   Do not execute the old unbounded scanner on this shape.
3. **Across-file cumulative budget:** two individually affordable source scans
   reuse ONE deliberately small budget; the second fails once cumulative owned
   strings exceed it. A newly reset budget must not be used in production.
   Prefer also exercise `generate` with two hash-bound synthetic selected files
   and a private test-only budget override to prove the actual loop shares it.
4. **Expanded class-header bypass and exact boundary:** the 1 MiB annotation-
   literal prefix produces a header-budget unresolved fact and zero confirmed
   methods while original annotation/file evidence remains. Construct full
   prefix spans exactly at the existing header limit and one byte beyond;
   supported exact boundary keeps owner, over-boundary does not.
5. **Writer limit and JSON escaping:** exact-boundary writes succeed, one byte
   beyond and a huge incoming chunk fail BEFORE len/capacity grows. Check
   newline inclusion and JSON escaping that expands beyond a small writer cap.
   Small pretty output matches `to_vec_pretty` plus newline exactly. Geometric
   capacity remains at or below the configured cap after many small writes.
6. **Generation/publication failure:** induce budget or serialization overflow
   using hash-bound synthetic source provenance and private test-only limit
   injection. `run` must fail without output/staging creation; in `--check`,
   previously valid output bytes are unchanged. No fixture source provenance is
   relaxed to make this test pass.

Private limit-injection helpers must keep the public production CLI limits fixed
and must not add a bypass flag. Tests assert actual error and state boundaries,
not timing thresholds or mirrored implementation formulas. All old 108 tests
must run unfiltered along with the new cases; no tests skipped/removed/weakened.

